# Menu module — Integration API guide for module authors

> You wrote (or plan to write) a `TgModuleContract` plugin and want it to
> appear in the bot's Telegram Mini App menu — with settings, actions,
> pickers, your own permission model, or a fully custom game screen.
> This guide shows every integration path. Normative source:
> `menu.md` §8 (contracts), §14.1 (chunk protocol), §18 (worked examples);
> this document is the developer-friendly rendering of the same rules.
> The menu ships as a **standalone composer dependency**
> (`bagart/telegram-bot-menu-module`) with its own repo and docs — nothing
> here requires you to touch host-app code.

## 0. Choose your track

| You want | Track | You write |
|---|---|---|
| A menu item + settings form | **Schema** (zero UI code) | manifest + `validate()` |
| Settings + "Run now"-style operations | **Schema + Actions** | manifest (+ optional webApi handler) |
| Your own screen inside the app | **Chunk** | prebuilt JS bundle + webApi handler |
| Chat/user pickers in your forms | **Resource fields** | nothing (hub-provided domains) |
| Your own RBAC ("Mafia moderator") | **Permission resolver** | one small contract impl |

All tracks compose: a chunk module can also ship schema-less settings via its
own handler, actions, and permissions.

## 1. Declare the dependency

```bash
composer require bagart/telegram-bot-menu-module
```

In your module class (`TgModuleContract`):

```php
public function requiresModules(): array { return ['menu' => '*']; }
```

Register through the standard registrar — the bootstrapper stamps ownership
(you never state your own module id):

```php
public function register(TgModuleRegistrar $r): void
{
    $r->webUi(MyUi::class);                       // required: menu entry
    $r->webApi(MyApi::class);                     // optional: custom API
    $r->webResource(MyPickerDomain::class);       // optional: picker domain
    $r->webPermissions(MyPerms::class);           // optional: your RBAC (§5)
}
```

Install/redeploy — done. No menu config, no routes, no frontend edits (INV-2).

## 2. Schema track — zero UI tax

```php
final readonly class MyUi implements TgWebUiContract
{
    public static function manifest(): TgWebUiManifest
    {
        return new TgWebUiManifest(
            moduleId: 'mystats',
            title: 't:title',                    // 't:' = i18n key
            icon: '📊',
            kind: UiKind::Tool,
            minAudience: UiAudience::Admin,
            entry: UiEntry::schema([             // hub renders everything
                new UiGroup('general', 'General', [
                    UiField::bool('enabled', 'Enabled', default: true),
                    UiField::int('threshold', 'Threshold', min: 1, max: 100),
                    UiField::resource('audience', 'Audience Chat',
                        resource: 'chat'),       // native picker sheet
                ]),
            ]),
            actions: [                           // declarative operations
                new UiAction('recount', 'Recount now', confirm: true),
            ],
            permission: 'mystats.view',          // optional, YOUR model (§5)
        );
    }

    public static function translations(): array
    {
        return ['en' => ['title' => 'My Stats'], 'ru' => ['title' => 'Моя статистика']];
    }
}
```

Settings round-trip: implement `TgSettingsFormContract` on the same or a
separate class:

```php
public function validate(array $raw): array;   // throw InvalidInputException{fieldErrors}
public function isConfigured(array $settings): bool;  // feeds the ⚠ badge
```

Rules that bite: no secret-capable field types exist (§8.5); `raw[key] = null`
deletes a leaf; lists replace wholesale; server-side validation is truth —
the schema only describes rendering. Save/conflict UX (version echo, 409
[Reload | Keep mine]) is built into the hub.

**Actions** always pair 1:1 with a declared route (§8.9 — the hub renders the
bar from the manifest alone):

```php
// In MyApi::routes():
new TgWebApiRoute(method: 'POST', path: 'actions/recount', minRole: EffectiveRole::Admin)
```

The hub POSTs to `/tgapp/api/v1/m/mystats/actions/recount`; return `{message}`
for a success toast. Danger flows: set `dangerText` for type-to-confirm styling.
Unpaired action id ⇒ doctor ERROR at refine time.

## 3. Custom API track

Implement `TgWebApiHandlerContract` when your screen needs endpoints:

```php
public static function routes(): array
{
    return [
        new TgWebApiRoute('POST', 'session/join', EffectiveRole::Member, ChatScope::Required),
        new TgWebApiRoute('GET',  'stats/{period}', EffectiveRole::Admin),
    ];
}

public function handle(TgWebRequest $request, array $path): TgWebResponse
{
    // $request->context is THE identity (bot/chat/module/role/user) — D36:
    // never read session/DB identity yourself; narrowing is allowed, widening is not.
    return TgWebResponse::ok([...]);
}
```

Paths are module-relative (`'stats/{period}'`, no leading `/`, no moduleId).
The dispatcher enforces method/minRole/chatScope/permission BEFORE your
handler runs; unmatched ⇒ 404 envelope; disabled module ⇒ 404
`module_disabled` while mounted chunks keep rendering.

## 4. Resource track (pickers)

Consume built-in domains by declaring a field `type: resource` with
`resource: 'chat' | 'user'` — the hub provides search/pagination/scoping.

Provide your own domain for other modules/forms:

```php
final class PersonaProvider implements TgResourceProviderContract
{
    public function domain(): string { return 'mystats.persona'; } // '<moduleId>.<name>'
    public function meta(): TgResourceMeta { ... }   // searchable/sortable/pagination flags
    public function search(TgResourceQuery $q, TgUiContext $ctx): TgResourcePage;   // context-scoped!
    public function validate(array $ids, TgUiContext $ctx): TgResourceSelection;    // per-id verdicts
}
```

Scoping is a security invariant (D39): identical queries under different
contexts MUST yield role-appropriate results — there is a dedicated test you
must pass.

## 5. Permissions — YOUR model, menu support

The hub knows only Telegram reality (member/admin/owner/superadmin). Anything
richer belongs to you. Register a resolver and reference permission ids from
your manifest entries, actions, routes, or resource meta:

```php
final class MyPerms implements TgPermissionResolverContract
{
    public static function permissions(): list<string> { return ['mystats.view', 'mystats.recount']; }

    public function resolve(array $ids, TgUiContext $ctx): array
    {
        // Your logic, your caches. Batched: at most ONE call per request.
        return array_fill_keys($ids, $this->can($ctx)); // unknown id => false, throw => all false
    }

    public function revision(): string { return (string) $this->counter(); } // D59: feeds the
    // bootstrap ETag so your access changes invalidate clients. Keep it O(1):
    // a counter row / Redis key / max(updated_at). Return '1' if static.
}
```

Semantics: effective access = hub-role check AND your verdict — a permission
narrows, never widens. Denied entries/actions are filtered server-side before
rendering and rejected at dispatch; the frontend never sees permission ids.
Fail-closed everywhere: throwing resolver ⇒ denied for the request + logged.

## 6. Chunk track — full custom screens

For games and rich tools: build a content-hashed bundle exposing
`window.TgMenu.mount`, declare `UiEntry::Chunk(url)` + required features:

```js
window.__TG_MENU_CHUNK__ = {id: 'mystats', api: 1,
                            features: ['context','navigation','resources','haptics','fullscreen']};
window.TgMenu = {mount(rootEl, bridge) {/* render; all HTTP via bridge.fetch */}};
```

The frozen surface (`ts/bridge.d.ts`, vendored next to `bridge-mock.ts`):
`fetch` (strict `/m/<module>` prefix), `navigate/close`, `setBackHandler`
(exclusive), `haptic`, `theme()`, `searchResources` (iff `'resources'`
granted), fullscreen trio (iff `'fullscreen'` granted). Credentials never
cross the bridge. Feature negotiation degrades gracefully — declare only what
you require. Local development without deploying: ask the host admin to add
your dev origin to `config('menu.chunks.dev_urls')` (never enabled in prod;
doctor enforces). Publish assets via your package's `<moduleId>-assets` tag.

## 7. Tooling

- `tg:menu:scaffold mystats` — generates a passing manifest skeleton + Pest case.
- `tg:menu:manifest mystats --json` — what the host actually sees (routes,
  masks, your declared permissions).
- `tg:menu:doctor` — integration linter: capability honesty, grammar lint,
  action⇄route pairing, resource scoping probe, permission declaration checks.
- `Testing\TgWebUiContractTest::for(MyUi::class)` — reusable Pest battery;
  run it in YOUR suite.

## 8. Stability promise & checklist

The `Contracts\` namespace is semver'd: additive is non-breaking; breaking =
new major + migration notes. Wire formats carry explicit versions
(`schemaVersion`, chunk `api`). Before opening your PR:

- [ ] `requiresModules()` contains `'menu'`
- [ ] Manifest passes `SchemaAssertions`; ids unique; `when` refs backward-only
- [ ] Every UiAction has a matching route (minRole sane)
- [ ] Resource domain namespaced `<moduleId>.…`; provider passes scoping test
- [ ] Permission ids declared ⇄ referenced (1:1); resolver total & batched
- [ ] No Laravel imports in contract-facing pure classes (INV-4)
- [ ] `tg:menu:doctor` clean for your module; contract battery green
